{
  "_about": "Pre-1911 (etymological) -> 1911 (post-reform) spelling pairs. These are the changes the Reforma de 1911 actually made: Greek-digraph simplification (ph/th/rh, ch=/k/, y->i), geminate-consonant reduction (keeping only etymological rr/ss), removal of etymological silent consonants in mn/ps clusters and of medial/initial silent h, and the regularisation of loanword k/w. Each modern form carries its correct AO1990 diacritics; where the European and Brazilian norms differ only by an open/closed vowel BEFORE A NASAL (anónimo/anônimo, cómodo/cômodo) the European form is stored and the Brazilian edge re-derives ê/ô by rule. IMPORTANT: the 1911 reform did NOT drop the (then-pronounced or PT-conventional) etymological c/p of -ct-/-pt- words like acto, óptimo, director, afecto -- those survived into the pre-AO1990 norm and are dropped only on the AO1990 edge (see base_iv.json). So 'affecto'->'afecto' (1911) is correct and distinct from 'afecto'->'afeto' (AO1990).",
  "_sources": [
    "Reforma Ortografica de 1911 (Portaria de 1 de Setembro de 1911), comissao de Goncalves Viana",
